const faq$1 = {"title":"Frequently Asked Questions","subtitle":"Practical information about Sanam Luang's facilities, history, and visit planning.","amenities":{"title":"Nearby Facilities","items":[{"icon":"🎟️","label":"Tickets","value":"The square is free"},{"icon":"🚻","label":"Restrooms","value":"Shops & museums nearby"},{"icon":"🏧","label":"Exchange / ATM","value":"Dense in nearby streets"},{"icon":"♿","label":"Accessibility","value":"Level, very gently sloped"}]},"categories":[{"id":"infra","title":"Transport & Infrastructure","items":[{"q":"What is the easiest way to reach Sanam Luang?","a":"Take the MRT Blue Line to Sanam Chai station — it puts you in the centre of the old town, about 10–15 minutes' walk from the square. Alternatively take a Chao Phraya express boat to Tha Chang (N9) or Tha Phra Chan pier and walk in. Old-town traffic is heavy, so driving is not recommended."},{"q":"Is there parking near the square?","a":"There is no dedicated car park, and old-town streets are narrow. Use paid parking at nearby shopping centres or office buildings and walk in. On holidays and ceremony days spaces are extremely tight."},{"q":"Are there toilets or drinking water on the square?","a":"The square itself has no public toilets or drinking fountains. The nearest are in convenience stores, malls and museums around the perimeter. Sort out supplies before you enter."},{"q":"Is it accessible with a wheelchair or pram?","a":"Walkways around the square are even and almost flat, and main entrances and surrounding pavements have kerb ramps, so wheelchairs and prams are broadly workable. There is no fixed seating or shade inside, however, and some pavements are uneven — allow extra time."}]},{"id":"geo","title":"History & Science","items":[{"q":"What is Sanam Luang?","a":"Sanam Luang (สนามหลวง) is a large oval public green space in Bangkok's old town, immediately north of the Grand Palace. It has been the core site of royal ceremony since the founding of Bangkok in 1782 and today serves national ceremonies, public events and everyday recreation."},{"q":"Where does the name 'Sanam Luang' come from?","a":"Sanam Luang translates literally as 'royal field': sanam means an open ground, luang means royal. Its earlier name was Thung Phra Men, the 'cremation field', because the ground was long used for royal cremations; it was renamed around 1855."}]},{"id":"plan","title":"Planning & Opening Information","items":[{"q":"Do I need a ticket for Sanam Luang?","a":"No. As a public green space the square is open all day, free, and you can walk onto the lawns and paths freely. Note that the Grand Palace and Wat Phra Kaew next door are separate ticketed sites with their own hours and dress code."},{"q":"How long should I spend there?","a":"A single circuit with photos takes about 1–1.5 hours. If you also take in the Grand Palace exterior, the National Museum or the City Pillar Shrine, allow half a day."},{"q":"When is the best time to visit?","a":"7–9 am and the 60 minutes before sunset are the two best windows: mornings are cool, quiet and cleanly lit, while the late light is warmer and brings out the Grand Palace roofs and lawns. Avoid the harsh 11 am – 3 pm sun."}]},{"id":"nearby","title":"Nearby Connections","items":[{"q":"What else can I visit nearby?","a":"The Grand Palace and Wat Phra Kaew are within walking distance to the south; the Bangkok National Museum, City Pillar Shrine and the way to Wat Pho lie west; Thammasat University and market streets to the north; Democracy Monument and the Tha Maharaj riverside quarter are north-east along Ratchadamnoen Avenue."}]},{"id":"entity","title":"Sanam Luang: Location & Essentials","items":[{"q":"Where is Sanam Luang?","a":"It is on Rattanakosin Island in Phra Nakhon district, Bangkok, at QF4V+88R, Ratchadamnoen Klang Rd, Phra Borom Maha Ratchawang, Phra Nakhon, Bangkok 10200 — immediately north of the Grand Palace, at 13.7544°N, 100.4908°E."},{"q":"What are the English and Thai names?","a":"English: Sanam Luang. Thai: สนามหลวง. Its former name was Thung Phra Men (ทุ่งพระเมรุ). In Chinese it is usually rendered 皇家田广场."},{"q":"What are the opening hours?","a":"As public green space the square is open all day with no admission fee. The adjacent Grand Palace and Wat Phra Kaew are separate ticketed sites, generally open during daytime hours and subject to temporary changes for royal ceremonies — check on-site notices."},{"q":"How is Sanam Luang rated?","a":"On its public Google Maps page, Sanam Luang holds a rating of about 4.6 out of 5 across more than 27,000 reviews, and is categorised as a State park. These figures are synced from the public page for reference only."}]}]};
const scienceArt$1 = {"title":"The Square & the City: How Sanam Luang Frames Old Bangkok","subtitle":"Look past the postcard view and two things stand out: a public lawn that has stayed empty for two centuries, and the palace-and-park skyline that the emptiness was kept to protect.","scienceMuseum":{"name":"Sanam Luang, the royal field","role":"Why the ground was left open","highlight":"What is unusual about Sanam Luang is not what stands on it but what does not. The ground north of the Grand Palace has been kept clear since the capital was founded in 1782, used for royal cremations, ceremonies and the ploughing rite rather than for building. Standing on the lawn, the emptiness reads as a deliberate civic decision: the square works as a foreground that makes the palace walls and the golden roofs behind them legible.","points":["Core: a royal field kept unbuilt since 1782, now the city's shared front lawn.","Function: ceremonial ground for state and royal rites, and a public park the rest of the year.","Detail: the low skyline is the point — nothing is allowed to block the view of the palace roofs."],"ctaLabel":"Tourism Authority of Thailand ↗","ctaUrl":"https://www.tourismthailand.org"},"artMuseum":{"name":"The old-town skyline","role":"Reading the view from the lawn","highlight":"From the middle of the square the view is a compressed history of Bangkok: the white palace walls and gilded roofs of Wat Phra Kaew to the south, the Ministry of Defence and the National Museum on the edges, and the towers of the modern city rising behind them. It is one of the few places in Bangkok where three centuries of architecture sit in a single frame.","points":["Best light: early morning from the north-west, when the palace roofs catch the first sun.","Read it in layers: palace walls, then colonial-era civic buildings, then the modern skyline.","Photography: the lawn is the viewpoint; the palace is the subject."],"ctaLabel":"Bangkok Metropolitan Administration ↗","ctaUrl":"https://www.bangkok.go.th"}};
const outdoorMuseum$1 = {"title":"Photography & Photo Guide: Sanam Luang's Best Spots","subtitle":"As Bangkok's most recognizable city square, a few structured spots and times greatly improve your photos' usefulness and beauty.","items":[{"id":"sunrise","icon":"🌅","name":"The lawn · towards the Grand Palace roofs","location":"South / west lawn","background":"From dawn to dusk the open lawn gives a clean foreground, with the golden roofs of the Grand Palace and Wat Phra Kaew beyond — Sanam Luang's classic 'lawn and golden roofs' composition, with the roof line sharpest against the light.","historicalLight":"Historical light: this lawn has been reserved for royal ceremony since Bangkok was founded in 1782. Early light rakes across it and turns the palace roofs into a row of golden silhouettes — gold that comes from centuries of regilding and repair, and looks most saturated against the light.","points":["Use a 16-35mm wide lens and let the walkway act as a leading line.","Use the walkway to draw the eye towards the palace roofs.","Crouch low so the lawn and sky layer up and the frame steadies."],"badge":{"value":"Morning","label":"Best light"}},{"id":"bridge","icon":"🌉","name":"Street corner · urban overlook","location":"Street corners around the square","background":"Shooting from the street side around the square packs traffic, the golden palace roofs and old-town buildings into one frame — one of Sanam Luang's most recognisable vantage points.","historicalLight":"Historical light: the old-town street grid took shape during the city's modernisation in the late 19th century, with several avenues meeting here. At dusk, warm light refracts off the glass of the older western buildings and mixes with passing headlights, setting 'modern speed' against 'historical order'.","points":["Bring a 70-200mm zoom to compress the street scene against the roofs.","Side light in the morning gives the roofs the softest texture.","Watch your footing and the crowds — never step into the roadway for a shot."],"badge":{"value":"All day","label":"Easiest"}},{"id":"sea","icon":"🌳","name":"Tamarind line · shade and people","location":"Tree belt at the square's edge","background":"The rows of century-old tamarinds along the edge are the oldest town's densest urban shade. Residents exercising beneath them, birds in the canopy and the open lawn together make up the local view of the square.","historicalLight":"Historical light: these tamarinds were planted during the late 19th-century improvements and are still standing. The canopy filters harsh afternoon sun into dappled patches on the grass — one of the few comfortable places to linger during the day.","points":["Use leaves as a natural frame for the palace roofs beyond.","Against the light, make the tree shadows the subject to show dapple and texture."],"badge":{"value":"Morning","label":"Most comfortable"}},{"id":"night","icon":"🌃","name":"Night scene and lights","location":"North / north-east of the square","background":"After sunset the square lights up and floodlights pick out the Grand Palace roofs, contrasting warm against the cool street lamps and traffic — a dependable vantage for city night photography.","historicalLight":"Historical light: by night the square shifts from ceremonial ground to a place for evening walks. During blue hour (20–40 minutes after sunset) the sky still holds blue while the palace floodlights are warm — the best balance for night shots.","points":["Use a tripod and a 10–30 second exposure for traffic and light trails.","Blue hour renders roof detail better than full darkness."],"badge":{"value":"Night","label":"Quietest"}}]};
const education$1 = {"title":"Why Is This Bangkok's Civic Landmark?","subtitle":"Sanam Luang is more than a green space: it is the spatial hinge of Bangkok's monarchy, civic life and history.","points":[{"title":"A square the city still uses every day","text":"Sanam Luang was not set aside as a monument to be looked at. It was kept open so that it could keep being used, and that is how it works today: ceremony on some days, a public lawn on the rest.","items":["Royal ceremony and everyday recreation share the same ground.","Ground kept unbuilt for over two centuries is itself the landmark."]},{"title":"Reading the palace walls from the lawn","text":"The square's real function is optical as well as ceremonial: it is the open foreground that lets the Grand Palace, Wat Phra Kaew and the surrounding civic buildings be seen as one continuous historic core.","items":["The low skyline is protected, so the palace roofs stay visible.","Walking the perimeter is the simplest way to read the old town."]},{"title":"Keeping royal history in public view","text":"Sanam Luang did not erase its royal and ceremonial background; it keeps that history in front of the public, so visitors sense what the ground has been used for while they are standing on it.","items":["History and everyday use happen in the same place.","A rare case of a ceremonial ground that is also an ordinary city park."]}]};
const plantGuide$1 = {"title":"Sanam Luang Through the Year","subtitle":"One public square that changes with the seasons: when the light is best, when the ground is coolest, and when the ceremony comes.","comparison":[{"icon":"🌸","badge":"Cool season","name":"Cool season & clear air","summary":"The cool season (Nov–Feb) is the most comfortable time to walk the square: moderate temperatures and clearer air, with the palace roofs at their sharpest against the sky.","points":["About Nov–Feb, roughly 20–30°C, the most comfortable window of the year.","Morning and dusk light is soft, best for photographs.","Clearest air of the year, so the palace roofs read sharpest."]},{"icon":"🍂","badge":"Hot season","name":"Hot season & kite weather","summary":"The hot season (Mar–May) is bright and clear but punishing at midday; dawn and dusk are the only comfortable windows, and this is also kite-flying season on the square.","points":["About Mar–May; dawn and dusk are the best times to visit.","Clear, transparent light with a high hit rate for photos.","Take sun protection and water — the middle of the square has no shade."]}],"seasonTitle":"Seasonal highlights","seasonSubtitle":"One square, two tempers. Below are the scenes most worth expecting in each season.","seasons":[{"icon":"🌱","badge":"COOL","name":"Cool · clear air & breeze","summary":"Before the heat builds, the square is at its most comfortable, which makes this the best season for slow visits and dawn photographs.","highlights":["Softest light at dawn and dusk.","The clearest views of the palace roofs all year.","Relaxed crowds, good for quiet walking."]},{"icon":"🌿","badge":"HOT","name":"Hot · kite season on the lawn","summary":"Bright, transparent light and a high, clear sky, but hot at midday; dawn and dusk are the comfortable windows, and kites fill the square.","highlights":["Kite-flying season, when the lawn itself becomes the attraction.","Dawn and dusk are the best times to visit.","Take sun protection and water."]},{"icon":"🍁","badge":"RAINY","name":"Rainy · showers & fresh light","summary":"The rainy season (Jun–Oct) brings brief, heavy showers; after the rain the air clears and the light on the palace roofs turns dramatic — a different, often emptier window on the square.","highlights":["The most dramatic light comes in the hour after a shower.","Smaller crowds than in the cool season.","The lawn can be waterlogged; stay on the paths."]},{"icon":"❄️","badge":"YEAR-ROUND","name":"Year-round · the open heart of the city","summary":"Sanam Luang is open all year and is the natural starting point for old Bangkok; dawn and dusk are always the best windows.","highlights":["Dawn, as the city wakes and the square is quietest.","Dusk, when the light turns the palace roofs warm.","Take sun protection and mind the traffic on the surrounding roads."]},{"icon":"🌾","badge":"SPECIAL","name":"May · Royal Ploughing Ceremony","summary":"Every May, Sanam Luang hosts the ancient Brahmanic Royal Ploughing Ceremony. Ceremonial oxen are used to read the coming harvest, underlining the country's agricultural roots.","highlights":["See Thailand's agricultural and royal heritage in a single morning.","Elaborate traditional costume and ritual.","Note: parts of the square may be closed while the ceremony is staged."]}]};
const audience$1 = {"title":"Who Sanam Luang Is For","subtitle":"Not \"you'll like it\", but how to walk it: where to start, what to pair it with, and when to come.","resonanceLabel":"Why it fits: ","routeLabel":"Suggested pairing: ","tipLabel":"Tip: ","items":[{"icon":"👨‍👩‍👧","group":"Families","resonance":"A flat, open, free lawn with room to run and the palace roofs as a backdrop — easy to combine with kite season in the hot months.","tip":"Come in the late afternoon, when the light softens and the ground has cooled; there is no shade in the middle of the square."},{"icon":"📷","group":"Photographers & Couples","resonance":"The lawn is the foreground and the palace the subject, so first light on the golden roofs across the open square is the frame most people come for.","tip":"Arrive about 30–45 minutes before sunrise for the empty-lawn shot; by mid-morning the tour groups have reached the palace."},{"icon":"🌿","group":"History & Culture Lovers","resonance":"One lawn ties together the palace, the National Museum, the City Pillar Shrine and the ploughing rite, so old Bangkok reads as a single system from here.","tip":"Walk the square once without a plan, then pick two of the surrounding sites; attempting all of them in one morning is exhausting in the heat."},{"icon":"🗺️","group":"First-time Visitors to Bangkok","resonance":"The easiest place in the old town to get your bearings: everything historic is within about a 20-minute walk, and the square itself costs nothing.","tip":"Use the lawn as a base between the Grand Palace and the riverside rather than crossing the old town twice."}]};
const lodging$1 = {"title":"Where to Stay Around Sanam Luang","subtitle":"Bangkok's old town has two practical bases for this square. We do not recommend specific hotels, but here is how the patterns differ.","scenarioTitle":"Two bases, how to choose","options":[{"icon":"🏛️","name":"Old town, beside the square","role":"Closest to the square","highlight":"Staying in the old town puts Sanam Luang and the Grand Palace within a short walk, which matters most if you want the square at first light before the coaches arrive.","commute":"To the square: about 5–15 minutes on foot, on flat walkable streets. The easiest option for a dawn visit.","points":["5–15 minutes on foot to the square — the closest you can stay.","Palace, museums and the City Pillar Shrine all within walking distance.","Quieter in the evening; fewer late-night food options."]},{"icon":"☕","name":"Khao San / riverside old town","role":"Best for food and transport links","highlight":"A short walk north-west of the square, this area keeps markets, cafes and street food on your doorstep, which suits travellers who want to eat late and still walk to the square in the morning.","commute":"To the square: roughly 15–25 minutes on foot, or a short taxi ride. Good for onward transfers.","points":["Street food, cafes and markets within a few minutes' walk.","Boat piers and bus links to the rest of the city.","Busier and noisier at night than the palace side."]},{"icon":"🛶","name":"Chao Phraya riverside","role":"Best if you are combining the river","highlight":"If Sanam Luang is one stop on a longer itinerary, staying near the Chao Phraya makes sense: express boats reach Tha Chang and Tha Phra Chan piers, both a short walk from the square.","commute":"To the square: about 15–25 minutes by taxi, or a boat to Tha Chang or Tha Phra Chan and a short walk.","points":["Express boat piers for the palace and the square.","Riverside views and easy connections north and south.","Slightly longer to reach the square on foot."]}],"peakTitle":"Peak-season warning","peak":"Bangkok's cool season (Nov–Feb) and public holidays tighten the old-town room supply and push prices up. Book weeks ahead; if you are travelling over a holiday, widen your search towards the Silom or Sukhumvit lines and take a taxi in.","tipsTitle":"Lodging tips","tips":["For a dawn visit to the square: stay in the old town on the palace side and walk in.","For food and convenience: choose the Khao San / riverside old town and eat within a few minutes of your room.","Before booking, confirm breakfast, air conditioning, and whether the street is quiet at night.","Over peak season and holidays, book early or widen the search area — old-town rooms sell out first."]};
